<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serpent Column</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/479-bce">479 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Serpent Column is a bronze column of three intertwined snakes that once supported a golden tripod dedicated at Delphi by the Greek states that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> in 479 BCE. Its coils bear the names of 31 allied states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where its damaged remains still stand in the Hippodrome, today's Sultanahmet Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serpent-column.jpg" alt="Serpent Column" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Serpent_Column.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Greek states that had fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> dedicated the monument to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi from the spoils of the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. Herodotus records that they set apart a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithe</a> for the god, and from it was made the tripod that rested on the bronze three-headed serpent, nearest to the altar. The golden tripod and cauldron, made from Persian weapons, are long lost. The bronze column of three intertwined snakes is about 8 metres (26 ft) high. Herodotus also records dedications to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> at the Isthmus.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and commander at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, had a dedication engraved on it that ascribed the victory to himself alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> cites the affair among the grounds for Spartan suspicion that he was ready to go over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. The Spartans removed the inscription and engraved the names of the cities that had taken part. Pseudo-Demosthenes gives a different account, in which the Plataeans brought a suit against Sparta before the Amphictyonic council. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> says a couplet by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a> replaced the dedication.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The travel writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> saw the bronze part at Delphi in the second century CE. The gold had been taken in 354 BCE by the Phocian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philomelus">Philomelus</a> to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-sacred-war">Third Sacred War</a>. Constantine the Great moved the column to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 324 to decorate the spina of the Hippodrome. Later it was made into a fountain with three mouths, and travellers described it from 1422. Ahmed Bican wrote that the citizens regarded it as a talisman against snake bites.</p><p>One serpent's lower jaw went missing in the decades after the Ottoman conquest. The accepted version says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> shattered it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> tells it so, though other Ottoman writers named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a>, Suleiman II or Murad IV. Petrus Gyllius in 1550 and Pietro Della Valle in 1614 mention no damage. According to the chronicler Silahdar Findiklili Mehmed Aga, in the Nusretname, the heads fell off on the night of 20 October 1700. An upper jaw is in the Istanbul Archaeology Museums.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The ground was raised in 1630 and hid the inscribed part. Charles Thomas Newton supervised the excavation of the base in 1855, which uncovered the inscription from the 13th coil to the 3rd. It was deciphered by C. Frick in 1856 and by Ernst Fabricius in 1886. The 13th coil reads 'Those who fought the war', and coils 12 to 3 give the names of 31 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, eight of them not named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> as present at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. The list suggests that the monument relates to the whole Persian war, not only to that battle. A bronze copy was set up at Delphi in 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Serpent Column” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Greek art and politics: the Athenian Treasury, the bronze Charioteer of Delphi of about 475 BCE, and the <b>Serpent Column</b> set up after the Battle of Plataea in 479 BCE, which Constantine the Great later moved to Constantinople, where its remains still stand in the old Hippodrome of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Some 31 states are named on the <b>Serpent Column</b> dedicated at Delphi after the war.</blockquote><a href="/state/hellenic-alliance">Hellenic Alliance</a></li></ul></section>
